3.0.2
Two measured bypasses of the A6 protected-branch gate, both reproduced end-to-end with controls in the same repo, and both the same shape one premise further out: a gate that keys on the FORM of a command is only as sound as the assumption that the form determines the effect. A6.2's catch-up exemption resolved a ref by NAME and trusted its VALUE — `git update-ref refs/remotes/origin/main <sha>` is ungated, and after it `git merge --ff-only origin/main` was ALLOWED and landed never-gated content on a protected branch while every configuration read came back honest, so the state check that answers the other finding does not answer this one. A NAME IS NOT A VALUE. Proving the ref fresh is not available either: `update-ref -m` forges any reflog message, the log is a file that can be removed, and a FRESH CLONE HAS NO REFLOG FOR THE TRACKING REF AT ALL, so a freshness check fails closed on every new clone — so the exemption is deleted and `git merge` on a protected branch is gated unconditionally, `--abort`/`--continue`/`--quit` excepted. Nothing is lost, because the contrast measurement says the catch-up route is `git pull --ff-only`: poison `origin/main`, pull, and the pull's own FETCH restores the ref before merging. The pull arm meanwhile trusted an upstream it never read — "targets the configured upstream by construction" was false, `branch.<cur>.merge` is re-pointable and re-pointing is ungated, so `git branch -u origin/rogue main && git pull --ff-only` landed a whole foreign branch on main — and now compares NAMES, which simultaneously un-gates `git pull --ff-only origin main`, the form a consumer reported as a false positive and which is STRICTLY MORE PROVABLY SAFE than the one that was allowed, because it names what it lands. Because that comparison reads mutable config, a gated pull/merge/push is also refused when an earlier clause moves what it resolves to (`branch -u`, `config branch.*|remote.*`, `update-ref`, `fetch` with a destination refspec, `remote set-url|remove|add`) or when the invocation ITSELF carries `-c`, `--config-env` or a `GIT_CONFIG_*` prefix — that last needs no chaining, so a rule about preceding clauses cannot see it, and `-c branch.main.remote=evil pull --ff-only` passes the name comparison honestly while pulling from another remote. THE BLOCKLIST IS KNOWN-INCOMPLETE AND SAYS SO: four resolution inputs times four-plus mutation channels, and the same channel behaves differently PER KEY — `-c branch.main.merge=` fails only because that key is multi-valued, so the cell matters, not the row or the column. The sound inverse (an allowlist of proven-inert preceding clauses) is queued, not snuck in, because it would gate `git checkout feature/z && git merge feature/y`, which v3.0.1 shipped on purpose. Deferred and named rather than rushed: `--git-dir`/`--work-tree` retarget the repo and are NOT resolved the way `-C` is, so a gated command run from a non-protected cwd against a protected repo still passes — pre-existing since v3.0.0, measured with its `-C` control, and queued.
